TASK 1 — DEMO SCRIPT (Explain Like I'm 5–7 Years Old)
Total speaking time: ~6 minutes

🎬 OPENING — "The Problem" (60 seconds)

Speak slowly, warm smile, make eye contact

"Imagine you run a bakery. 🍰
Every day, customers walk in and say — 'I want a cake that looks like a sunset over mountains.'
And you think — that sounds amazing! But here's the problem. You only have one master baker. One person. And making that exact cake takes time, skill, and special ingredients only your baker knows.
Now imagine hundreds of customers coming in every day — all wanting different, beautiful, custom cakes.
Your one baker... is exhausted. Customers are waiting. Some leave. Some complain.
That's the exact problem our product solves — but instead of cakes, we're creating AI-generated images.
Right now, there's a powerful AI image kitchen run by Google — called Flow. It can make stunning images from just a sentence. But only the person who owns the kitchen can use it. There's no way to let your customers order from it — through your own app, with your own brand.
Until today."

⚙️ MIDDLE — "How It Works" (3 minutes)

Now show the screen — Electron app open, ready to generate

"Let me show you what we built.
Think of our system like a magical restaurant with three rooms. 🏠
Room 1 — The Front Desk (point to Electron app dashboard)
This is the beautiful counter where customers walk in. They see your brand — your name, your colors. They type in what they want. In our demo, I'll type: 'a red apple on a white table.'
They press the button. That's it. They don't see anything complicated. Just a clean, beautiful screen.
(Click Generate)
Room 2 — The Kitchen (narrate while image generates)
Behind the scenes — invisible to the customer — our system does something remarkable.
It's like a very well-trained robot butler 🤖 who knows a secret passageway to Google's AI kitchen.
The butler walks through that passageway, goes to the kitchen, whispers your customer's order, waits while the kitchen creates the image, then picks up the finished picture and runs back through the passageway — all in about 30 seconds.
The customer never sees the passageway. They never see the kitchen. They just see... (wait for image to appear) ...
their image. Ready. Done. Beautiful.
(Image appears on screen)
Room 3 — The Delivery Counter (point to completed image)
The customer sees their image. They can download it. They can generate another one. They never had to log in to Google. They never had to pay Google. They never even know Google was involved.
All they know is — your app made them something magical.

Now, here's what makes this a business, not just a toy.
Imagine that same restaurant — but instead of one table, you have 100 customers ordering at the same time.
Our system has what we call a ticket queue — think of it like a deli counter number system. 🎫
Each customer gets a number. The kitchen handles them one at a time. Nobody crashes into each other. Nobody's order gets lost. Everyone gets their result — in order, perfectly delivered.
And our system has a night watchman 🌙 — a little program that checks every 10 minutes to make sure the passageway to Google's kitchen is still open. If it ever closes, he reopens it quietly. Customers never feel a thing."

🎯 CLOSING — "What's Better Now" (90 seconds)

Smile, speak with confidence

"So let's recap what just happened.
Before this POC:
❌ You couldn't offer AI image generation to your own customers.
❌ Every customer would need their own Google account.
❌ You had no branded experience — just sending people to someone else's website.
❌ Zero control. Zero brand. Zero business.
After this POC:
✅ Your customers open your app.
✅ They type a sentence.
✅ A real AI image appears — powered by Google, but delivered by you.
✅ Your brand. Your control. Your revenue.
This is a Proof of Concept — meaning we've proven the idea works. The restaurant is open, the kitchen is connected, and the butler knows the route.
The next step is simply making it bigger — adding payments, adding more customers, adding more features.
The hard part? Already done. Today. Right here.
Thank you."
